  "text": "In a small series of seven individuals with seasonal nasal allergy, the investigators looked for common mental patterns, especially a lingering focus on smells. One participant who experienced a severe attack before the onset of a sudden nasal inflammation described hidden thoughts that emerged during therapy. These thoughts involved a blocked sexual interest related to reproduction, which the person tried to redirect into visual and intellectual activities. The effort was intense, but the visual channel proved inadequate because the underlying curiosity was too aggressive, prompting a shift toward a more basic, smell‑related interest. Dream reports showed that external odors continued to trigger conflict, creating a self‑reinforcing cycle that was briefly broken when the person developed a conversion symptom—nasal blockage that reduced both smell and visual perception. By comparing the unconscious material from all seven cases, the authors suggest that particular inner conflicts may be linked to allergic episodes, though it remains unclear whether an innate heightened sense of smell leads to a regressive coping strategy or whether the regression itself heightens smell sensitivity. A principle that combines internal and external stimuli may help explain how psychological factors interact with allergic reactions."
